drm/panfrost: do not map imported buffers cacheable

panfrost_mmu_map() sets IOMMU_CACHE unconditionally, for every buffer
object it maps, including ones imported from another driver over dma-buf.
That is an assumption panfrost is not entitled to make: the memory
attributes of an imported buffer belong to whoever allocated it, and
nothing in the dma-buf interface reports what they are.

On ARM_MALI_LPAE, IOMMU_CACHE selects ARM_LPAE_MAIR_ATTR_IDX_CACHE, which
the Mali MAIR (arm_mali_lpae_alloc_pgtable) defines as
ARM_MALI_LPAE_MEMATTR_WRITE_ALLOC -- Normal, write-back, write-allocate.
Meanwhile any drm_gem_dma exporter on a non-coherent platform allocated
that memory with dma_alloc_wc(), so the CPU has it mapped Normal
Non-cacheable, and the display engine reading it snoops nothing at all.

Aliasing one physical address with mismatched cacheability is CONSTRAINED
UNPREDICTABLE on ARM rather than merely slow. The two views are not
coherent, and a dirty line belonging to the cacheable view can be written
back over data the non-cacheable agent wrote, at a moment neither of them
controls.

This is reachable on any "split renderer" system -- a display-only KMS
device with no render node, paired with a separate GPU -- which is the
whole reason kmsro exists. It is how this board runs: mesa allocates the
scanout buffer as a dumb buffer on the display device (drm_gem_dma, CMA,
write-combine), exports it, and imports it into panfrost, which then maps
it write-back. Confirmed on hardware from /sys/kernel/debug/dma_buf/bufinfo:
four 3,723,264-byte buffers exported by drm and attached only to
1800000.gpu, while neither the GPU nor the display node declares
dma-coherent.

Drop IOMMU_CACHE for imported objects. That selects ATTRINDX 0, which the
Mali MAIR defines as MEMATTR_IMP_DEF, matching the non-cacheable view the
exporter and the CPU already have. Natively allocated buffers keep
IOMMU_CACHE and are entirely unaffected, so the tiler heap and ordinary
render targets are not slowed down; the only buffers that change are
imports, which on this path are written once per frame and read by
scanout hardware that was never going to see a cache anyway.

Being strictly correct would mean asking the exporter for its coherency
rather than assuming either answer, but dma-buf has no such query today,
and non-cacheable is the safe assumption: getting it wrong that way costs
performance, while getting it wrong the other way is undefined behaviour.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_mmu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_mmu.c
@@ -438,6 +438,31 @@
 	if (bo->noexec)
 		prot |= IOMMU_NOEXEC;
 
+	/*
+	 * An imported buffer's memory attributes belong to whoever allocated
+	 * it, and nothing in the dma-buf interface reports what they are. We
+	 * must not assume they are cacheable.
+	 *
+	 * IOMMU_CACHE on ARM_MALI_LPAE selects ARM_LPAE_MAIR_ATTR_IDX_CACHE,
+	 * which the Mali MAIR defines as MEMATTR_WRITE_ALLOC -- Normal,
+	 * write-back, write-allocate. Exporters that allocated with
+	 * dma_alloc_wc() -- every drm_gem_dma driver on a non-coherent
+	 * platform, which is what a display engine feeding a panel typically
+	 * is -- have the same physical pages mapped Normal Non-cacheable by
+	 * the CPU, and the display reads them without snooping anything.
+	 *
+	 * Aliasing one physical address with mismatched cacheability is
+	 * CONSTRAINED UNPREDICTABLE on ARM, not merely slow: the two views
+	 * are not coherent, and a dirty line can be written back over data
+	 * the other agent wrote, at a time neither agent controls.
+	 *
+	 * So drop IOMMU_CACHE for imports, which selects ATTRINDX 0 and
+	 * MEMATTR_IMP_DEF, matching the non-cacheable view the exporter and
+	 * the CPU already have. Native BOs are unaffected and stay cached.
+	 */
+	if (drm_gem_is_imported(obj))
+		prot &= ~IOMMU_CACHE;
+
 	sgt = drm_gem_shmem_get_pages_sgt(shmem);
 	if (WARN_ON(IS_ERR(sgt)))
 		return PTR_ERR(sgt);
